  Cochran-Armitage trend z = +11.72  (two-sided p = 1.03e-31)
  logistic slope on age    = +0.1539  (SE 0.0133, z = +11.54)  -> OR per year = 1.166
  between-cohort heterogeneity chi2 = 433.5 on 10 df (a pure binomial would give ~10)
  Spearman(age, rate) rho = +0.527  (n=11, unweighted -- shown for shape only)

  NOTE ON THE FILTER: the deleted cohorts are the OLDEST and their pooled
  rate (23.3%) is about twice the corpus rate (11.6%). The authors' filter
  therefore removes evidence FOR the association their own figure shows,
  and the trend statistic gets stronger when the filter is undone.

========================================================================
HOW MUCH CLUSTERING DOES IT TAKE TO KILL THE TREND?
========================================================================
  Notebooks are not independent: every notebook in a repository shares
  one conda environment, and 76.4% of the corpus lives in repositories
  holding ten or more notebooks. Mean cluster size over the executed set:
      10389 executed notebooks / 2660 repositories = 3.91
  (an upper bound on the effective n -- not every repo contributed)

  DEFF = 1 + (mbar - 1) * ICC. Dividing every count by DEFF is the
  standard first-order correction for a clustered binomial.

     ICC     DEFF        trend z          p
    0.00     1.00          11.72   1.03e-31
    0.10     1.29          10.31   6.05e-25
    0.25     1.73           8.92   4.74e-19
    0.50     2.45           7.48   7.32e-14
    0.75     3.18           6.57   4.97e-11
    1.00     3.91           5.93   3.04e-09

  Even at ICC = 1 -- every notebook in a repository sharing one fate --
  the trend survives. The direction of this result is not a clustering
  artifact. What clustering DOES undermine is any confidence interval
  the authors' notebook-level counts would imply.

========================================================================
SYNTHETIC CONTROL -- no age effect, clustered like the real corpus
========================================================================
  2000 simulations, cohort sizes and overall rate matched to the data.
  naive |z| > 1.96 in 423/2000 = 21.1% of runs (nominal 5%)
  simulated |z| 95th percentile = 2.96   (the real data give z = +11.72)
  -> clustering inflates the naive test badly, and the observed z is still
     outside the inflated null. The sign is real; the p-value is not.

